WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.880
People say you're Satoshi.

2
00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:08.260
I got an email from him in August 2008.

3
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:10.240
That was the first email he sent anybody.

4
00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:11.820
What was the email about?

5
00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:13.660
He was asking, is this...

6
00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:14.940
I sent him a few...

7
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:17.000
He exchanged a few emails suggesting...

8
00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:21.140
From the academic side, when they saw Bitcoin, they were dismissive.

9
00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:26.480
If I could go back in time and told you that Bitcoin will be 100k one day,

10
00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:27.600
would you have believed me?

11
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.700
Got the whole crypto world going wild.

12
00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:36.260
Moneyball change, she's the queen of the game.

13
00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:39.620
Stacking crypto like it's her claim to fame.

14
00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:42.680
If you want success, then take a look away.

15
00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:47.680
Okay, Mom.

16
00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:49.500
Hi, Adam.

17
00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:50.780
Hi.

18
00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:56.780
You're in Taipei.

19
00:00:57.380 --> 00:00:57.680
I am.

20
00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:01.560
You went to the National Palace Museum in Taipei.

21
00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:01.940
Yeah.

22
00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:02.980
How was it?

23
00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:03.960
It was cool.

24
00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:05.680
Saw...

25
00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:09.080
Well, because Blockstream makes the jade hardware wallet,

26
00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:13.920
we saw almost a whole floor with different jade artifacts,

27
00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:21.500
knives and boxes and jewelry and all kinds of different things, actually.

28
00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:25.300
So we were learning more things about the material.

29
00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:30.720
Apparently, it's much harder and tougher than I was thinking it might be.

30
00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:31.940
Why is it called jade?

31
00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:38.040
The Blockstream jade is called jade because we have a wallet, a green wallet,

32
00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:39.820
which is a Bitcoin and liquid.

33
00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:41.660
Liquid is a Bitcoin layer too.

34
00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:44.280
So the wallet is called green.

35
00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:46.560
There's a reason it's called green,

36
00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:50.580
which is there was an older Bitcoin concept called a green address.

37
00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:53.900
So originally, the wallet was called green address,

38
00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:56.740
but it got shortened to Blockstream green.

39
00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:00.700
And then when we were making a hardware wallet,

40
00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:03.080
we wanted something connected with green.

41
00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:06.640
So jade is traditionally green, right?

42
00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:09.160
That's the most famous color for a jade gemstone.

43
00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:10.520
That's a beautiful name.

44
00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:13.240
I think a lot of people are very curious about you

45
00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:16.000
because people are calling you the godfather, the legend.

46
00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:18.320
And I am really curious.

47
00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:20.280
So you grew up in London.

48
00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:23.160
How does the legend's childhood look like?

49
00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:28.580
I was somebody who liked mathematics and got a computer,

50
00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:29.560
like a home computer.

51
00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:33.820
So for somebody interested in mathematics and science and things like that,

52
00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:37.500
a computer is quite an interesting thing to explore

53
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:38.640
and learn how to program it.

54
00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:43.720
So I taught myself basic and then assembly code,

55
00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:47.320
machine code on this computer, which is the low-level programming.

56
00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:50.240
So it's quite an interesting experience.

57
00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:54.340
And so when I did the next level of education in the UK

58
00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:56.720
is between 16 to 18.

59
00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:57.920
It's A-levels, they call it.

60
00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:04.680
And so there was no computer programming taught at this time, right?

61
00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:06.080
This was in 1989.

62
00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:11.100
But when I went after that, 18 to 21, the university degree,

63
00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:13.780
there was programming, like software engineering.

64
00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:15.000
So I did software.

65
00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:20.840
But it kind of connected back to teaching myself about programming when I was younger.

66
00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:24.240
You reserve engineer video games, I saw.

67
00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:25.100
Yeah.

68
00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:31.960
Well, it was something interesting to do on these computers to see if you could modify the game.

69
00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:40.460
So there were ways you could interrupt the game so that you could get into some kind of programming level.

70
00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:47.320
And then try modifying different, look through the memory for things that look like maps.

71
00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:56.420
And so if you could find a map and I guess the format, you could, you know, add extra objects or extra sprites into a room.

72
00:03:56.580 --> 00:04:00.860
So you could fill the room up with sprites and it would overload.

73
00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:01.960
So it would go very slow.

74
00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:03.760
So you do fun things with it, basically.

75
00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:07.780
Just trying to modify the game or work out what it's doing.

76
00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:08.920
Wow.

77
00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:14.760
The reason why people call you the legend is because you invented Hashcash.

78
00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:21.300
And Hashcash inspired Satoshi and the POW Bitcoin mining process.

79
00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:23.320
It's so crazy.

80
00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:24.740
Like, can you tell us the story?

81
00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:36.480
So how I did that is at university I got interested in encryption and digital signatures and things you could build with that.

82
00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:47.920
So the reason I found it interesting was I was aware of the encryption process because a friend of mine at university was implementing it as a kind of master's project.

83
00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:49.280
So explain it to me.

84
00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:54.720
And then PGP, the software for encrypting email, was released.

85
00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:58.660
But it had social implications.

86
00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:10.420
So it could do something novel and interesting where individuals could encrypt an email and a government, even a spy agency, couldn't decrypt it, which was like a very novel concept.

87
00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:17.980
And changed the capabilities for individuals to organize and communicate without government interfering.

88
00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:21.560
And of course, like the internet itself had some of that phenomena, right?

89
00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:31.780
When people were first able to self-publish or, you know, run a blog like you do, that was something upsetting for big media companies and the government.

90
00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:33.620
So it was something in that direction.

91
00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:40.880
But even more, I thought that was interesting and tried to find out if there were other ideas like that.

92
00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:48.200
And so then I found a cypherpunks list and they were using encryption to make anonymous email.

93
00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:55.960
The way it worked is that people would, volunteers would run servers to help make the emails anonymous.

94
00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:57.760
So I ran one of those.

95
00:05:57.980 --> 00:06:01.140
And what I was finding is that people would spam through it.

96
00:06:01.580 --> 00:06:11.880
And normally if you get spam, what they would do is try to block the sender, like block the server they were sending their mail from or block their email address.

97
00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:14.540
But because it's anonymous, you can't block it.

98
00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:16.420
You don't know who sent it by design, right?

99
00:06:16.540 --> 00:06:19.200
So I had to think of another solution.

100
00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:22.980
So I thought, well, the problem is it's free.

101
00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:24.360
There's no cost to sending email.

102
00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:26.840
So I tried to find a way to create a cost to sending an email.

103
00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:33.880
And of course, it's, you know, any kind of online payment is not suitable for micropayments.

104
00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:35.660
You know, this was before PayPal.

105
00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:43.040
It was very difficult to get the ability to process a credit card that you need a business and you would need to do some things.

106
00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:45.340
So for the average person, they couldn't receive a payment.

107
00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:48.600
And anyway, that wouldn't be private.

108
00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:50.040
I know I like something private.

109
00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:54.920
So I found a way to create a cost.

110
00:06:55.280 --> 00:07:00.840
So make a stamp by doing some work on your computer for a few minutes that the recipient could verify.

111
00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:03.420
And that was like a digital postage stamp.

112
00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:04.940
So it had a cost.

113
00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:09.180
So I published that on the list, like implemented it, published source code.

114
00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:16.740
And it caused people to realize that this was like digital gold because it was scarce.

115
00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:20.700
Most digital things are infinitely copyable, right?

116
00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:26.320
People would copy music, copy videos, and there's almost nothing anybody could do to stop them with file sharing.

117
00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:29.000
So finally, there was something hard to copy.

118
00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:39.180
And so basically, that started many years of people trying to work out how to make electronic cash using proof of work.

119
00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:42.360
But it was actually hard to make it work.

120
00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:52.060
There are technical complications of coordinating on the internet to prevent it being spent twice and things like that.

121
00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:55.020
So there were a few ideas.

122
00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:57.640
Again, I published it in 97.

123
00:07:57.940 --> 00:08:00.240
By 1998, there were a few ideas.

124
00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:03.520
People might have heard about B-Money or Bitgold.

125
00:08:03.820 --> 00:08:04.820
But they weren't implementable.

126
00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:05.620
They were just ideas.

127
00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:13.360
And so it wasn't until Satoshi released Bitcoin that somebody had found a solution to these problems.

128
00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:16.160
And he didn't discuss it with people first.

129
00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:18.640
He just implemented it and then released it.

130
00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:23.900
I got an email from him in August 2008.

131
00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:26.360
But I think that was the first email he sent anybody.

132
00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:29.460
And of course, I didn't realize that it was a pseudonym.

133
00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:30.720
I thought that was his name, right?

134
00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:32.400
What was the email about?

135
00:08:32.780 --> 00:08:34.240
He was using Hashcash.

136
00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:37.400
And I had it implemented in a paper describing it.

137
00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:39.940
So he was writing a paper.

138
00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:43.800
I think he'd already implemented Bitcoin, the software, or a beta version.

139
00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:46.760
And he was writing a paper to explain it to release.

140
00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:49.140
And so he was citing.

141
00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:51.760
He wanted to know how to cite it.

142
00:08:52.060 --> 00:08:56.720
Because it wasn't like the Hashcash paper wasn't published in an academic publication.

143
00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:57.860
It was just on my website.

144
00:08:58.120 --> 00:09:01.180
So he was asking, is this the correct way to cite it?

145
00:09:01.180 --> 00:09:09.180
I sent him a few emails suggesting some other systems that he might want to read or look at.

146
00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:11.180
One of them was B-Money.

147
00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:14.200
So he actually cited that as well.

148
00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:15.320
But I think it was me.

149
00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:16.560
He didn't seem to be aware of it.

150
00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:20.160
So I think it was me that ended up giving him something else to look at,

151
00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:23.640
which he realized was very similar to Bitcoin.

152
00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:24.720
So he cited it.

153
00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:27.360
What was the impression you got from the email?

154
00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:28.440
Did he say thank you?

155
00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:29.720
Was he like super nice?

156
00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:34.740
Yeah, it was just polite and focused on the point.

157
00:09:35.380 --> 00:09:40.360
And actually, I didn't release the emails for a long time.

158
00:09:40.540 --> 00:09:46.400
But I released them as part of the COPA trial earlier in the year.

159
00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:47.360
So they're online now.

160
00:09:47.900 --> 00:09:51.000
It's just like five or six emails, something like that.

161
00:09:51.020 --> 00:09:51.580
So not very long.

162
00:09:51.580 --> 00:09:55.860
When the Bitcoin white paper came out, what was the reaction from the community?

163
00:09:56.160 --> 00:09:57.360
And what was your impression?

164
00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:03.560
The original electronic cash paper was by David Chawm in 1985, I would say.

165
00:10:03.920 --> 00:10:07.320
Over 20 years by the time that Bitcoin is released, right?

166
00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:11.620
And there were like hundreds of papers about how to do this.

167
00:10:11.660 --> 00:10:16.560
And each one of them was trying to improve it a little bit or, you know, make something better.

168
00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:20.880
And so they had a very fixed idea of how it should work.

169
00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:24.000
And Bitcoin didn't work anything like these papers.

170
00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:27.620
So from the academic side, when they saw Bitcoin, they were dismissive.

171
00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:31.240
Because they're like, you know, they assumed it should have a central server.

172
00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:34.000
They assumed it should have very strong privacy.

173
00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:35.080
And it didn't.

174
00:10:35.740 --> 00:10:42.020
Now, there's a reason why it might not, which is it's hard to make that kind of privacy when there's no central server.

175
00:10:42.020 --> 00:10:48.540
And so you could see that, you know, you couldn't say, well, Satoshi should have used that because it doesn't work in a centralized system.

176
00:10:48.700 --> 00:10:49.780
But that was their reaction.

177
00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:51.240
They were like, oh, it's not good.

178
00:10:51.700 --> 00:10:58.260
So the academics were very dismissive for, I don't know, maybe four or five years until Bitcoin became bigger.

179
00:10:58.260 --> 00:11:02.980
And they realized, well, you know, their 23 years of ideas never went anywhere.

180
00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:04.200
And this is going somewhere.

181
00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:05.700
So maybe they should look at it.

182
00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:13.600
And for me, I was, because I was more doing applied things, I was aware of those electronic cache systems.

183
00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:16.560
So I thought it was unfortunate that it didn't have as much privacy as them.

184
00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:17.560
But I understood why not.

185
00:11:17.980 --> 00:11:20.240
The main question I had was, will it bootstrap?

186
00:11:20.620 --> 00:11:24.080
Because I tried out some of the earlier centralized systems.

187
00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:26.840
And they didn't get mass adoption.

188
00:11:27.260 --> 00:11:31.800
And actually, the Digicash one was a company in the Netherlands.

189
00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:35.100
And it failed financially.

190
00:11:35.700 --> 00:11:37.980
So that was the end of it, right?

191
00:11:38.020 --> 00:11:38.620
And it was centralized.

192
00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:40.740
So that was the end of the coins.

193
00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:42.320
You couldn't verify them anymore.

194
00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:45.340
So my main question was, like, will it bootstrap?

195
00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:47.440
You know, will enough people become interested and use it?

196
00:11:47.440 --> 00:12:00.720
You can see from the emails published by Marty Malmy, who was working with Stoshi on documentation and websites and a bit of code after it was released,

197
00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:07.140
that really, it didn't look like people were using it heavily.

198
00:12:07.140 --> 00:12:12.200
So there's another guy who was mining in 2009, Dustin Trammell.

199
00:12:12.380 --> 00:12:15.100
He did a podcast explaining the experience.

200
00:12:15.100 --> 00:12:17.700
So he mined it for, I don't know, some months.

201
00:12:17.780 --> 00:12:19.800
And then he got bored and left for a few years.

202
00:12:20.660 --> 00:12:25.040
And he runs a VC firm called Trammell Ventures now, investing in Bitcoin companies.

203
00:12:25.780 --> 00:12:29.740
But he was explaining what it was like, you know, that they were just hobbyists.

204
00:12:29.860 --> 00:12:31.580
There was no price, no value.

205
00:12:32.300 --> 00:12:35.360
They would exchange coins just to test it, just to have fun, right?

206
00:12:35.560 --> 00:12:39.160
So, I mean, I wasn't actively doing that.

207
00:12:39.220 --> 00:12:43.420
I just read some things and thought, okay, let's wait and see if it bootstraps.

208
00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:44.480
And eventually it did, right?

209
00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:48.580
People got used to the fact that it works in a different way, you know?

210
00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:56.580
Like the security comes from miners, sort of honest miners competing versus dishonest ones.

211
00:12:56.700 --> 00:12:59.740
So it's kind of like an arms race, 50-50 sort of.

212
00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:04.080
But you make more money generally if you mine honestly than dishonestly.

213
00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:11.960
With a central server, it's extremely secure and hard to attack unless the server's dishonest.

214
00:13:11.960 --> 00:13:14.800
Whereas with Bitcoin, there is no server.

215
00:13:15.080 --> 00:13:20.180
So it's not possible for any central party to become dishonest or fail and kill the system.

216
00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:25.740
But the security level is sort of more even kind of thing.

217
00:13:25.860 --> 00:13:30.520
So it took people a while to accept that that is okay.

218
00:13:30.520 --> 00:13:37.520
If I could go back in time and told you that Bitcoin will be 100k one day, would you have believed me?

219
00:13:39.020 --> 00:13:42.740
I mean, I would have been okay with it being worth a dollar when I read it.

220
00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:49.880
I mean, I was thinking because the previous system, the DigiCash system, they ran a demo server.

221
00:13:49.880 --> 00:13:53.860
There was no way to send money into it, but they ran it.

222
00:13:54.200 --> 00:13:57.880
And they promised they would never issue more than 1 million coins.

223
00:13:58.380 --> 00:14:10.580
And so myself and some of the people on the Cypherpunks list thought, well, maybe we can force it to have a value by selling things for it, just treating them as a dollar.

224
00:14:10.580 --> 00:14:19.060
So, you know, I sold some t-shirts that I was making with encryption software on and other people sold other things.

225
00:14:19.420 --> 00:14:25.040
But the problem was the company went bankrupt, you know, like not very many weeks into this experiment.

226
00:14:25.040 --> 00:14:38.760
So when I was saying I'll wait to see if it bootstrapped, like if people would adopt it, I got more active in 2013 because I think the price had got to $100.

227
00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:45.680
And I was like, okay, I knew it had already gone past somewhere, but like $100, okay, there's no arguing that's bootstrapped, right?

228
00:14:45.820 --> 00:14:52.520
So then I spent a lot of time doing what people do, go down the rabbit hole and read everything, try and learn more about it.

229
00:14:52.520 --> 00:14:56.480
And people say you're Satoshi.

230
00:14:57.300 --> 00:14:58.180
No, it's not me.

231
00:14:58.180 --> 00:15:06.680
I mean, I understand why they look for people who were, you know, programmers interested in launching a cache before.

232
00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:14.240
And of course, there are going to be some number of people like that just because they found it interesting, right?

233
00:15:15.620 --> 00:15:17.400
And I happen to be one of them, but it's not me.

234
00:15:17.400 --> 00:15:25.700
I always wonder, so we have AI now and we can launch spaceship in the space, but we cannot figure out who Satoshi is.

235
00:15:25.780 --> 00:15:26.460
How is that possible?

236
00:15:27.220 --> 00:15:32.620
Well, I think he must have used anonymity systems, right?

237
00:15:32.780 --> 00:15:34.720
Like Tor and things like that.

238
00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:44.720
Because, you know, there are other people that tried not very hard to be anonymous and they made mistakes and they got caught, like they got identified.

239
00:15:44.920 --> 00:15:46.900
Or they were doing something like the Silk Road guy.

240
00:15:47.360 --> 00:15:53.460
He made a straight up mistake and posted under his own name about Silk Road and then used to sue them afterwards.

241
00:15:53.600 --> 00:16:00.540
So, of course, as soon as somebody found the post under his own name, all the guessing was gone, right?

242
00:16:00.540 --> 00:16:06.720
In the case of Bitcoin, like people tried for many years to look through all of the forum posts, try and guess.

243
00:16:07.180 --> 00:16:11.920
And clearly there's no information because people would have found it by now.

244
00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:15.540
And I think he stopped participating.

245
00:16:17.040 --> 00:16:21.340
You know, by the time I read all the forum posts in 2013, he'd left already by a couple of years.

246
00:16:21.420 --> 00:16:23.040
I think he stopped somewhere in 2011.

247
00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:30.480
So, you know, if he's not communicating, there's no more emails, there's no more forum posts, there's no new information.

248
00:16:31.040 --> 00:16:32.860
So I think we're probably never going to know.

249
00:16:33.640 --> 00:16:34.840
And it's better for Bitcoin.

250
00:16:35.300 --> 00:16:36.840
Yeah, I think it is.

251
00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:46.120
I was curious if he did that by design, you know, if he thought it'd be better for Bitcoin or maybe he just thought it's risky.

252
00:16:46.120 --> 00:16:46.520
Oh.

253
00:16:48.280 --> 00:16:49.820
Well, it's also a timing thing.

254
00:16:49.940 --> 00:17:03.780
So Gavin Andreessen, who was one of the developers early on, announced that he had accepted to go and give a talk at the CIA, like the intelligence agency in the US.

255
00:17:04.500 --> 00:17:09.880
And people were surprised that he would do that because it's kind of, you know, maybe they wouldn't like it.

256
00:17:09.880 --> 00:17:16.100
And so it wasn't long after that Satoshi stopped participating on the forum.

257
00:17:18.100 --> 00:17:18.900
So I don't know.

258
00:17:18.980 --> 00:17:20.460
It's not clear if that was the reason.

259
00:17:20.760 --> 00:17:27.040
But I think in any case, if you wanted to be anonymous, you'd have to stop at some point.

260
00:17:27.300 --> 00:17:31.580
The more you would keep going, eventually you might make a mistake.

261
00:17:31.580 --> 00:17:35.800
Or people might care to try and identify him.

262
00:17:36.460 --> 00:17:42.440
And there's more risk if you're using online protocols like Tor.

263
00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:45.460
There's actually a live connection through the network, right?

264
00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:57.040
So if somebody's trying to identify you while you're using it, it's much more possible for them to find who's on the other end of the connection than if it's all gone and it's in history.

265
00:17:57.040 --> 00:17:58.840
Because that live connection isn't there anymore.

266
00:17:58.960 --> 00:18:01.160
So you can't try and search for it while it's being used.

267
00:18:02.020 --> 00:18:10.160
So, yeah, I'm thinking another reason he could stop is that it's become more widely known and there are more users.

268
00:18:10.460 --> 00:18:14.040
So it's more likely for there to be interest in identifying him.

269
00:18:15.140 --> 00:18:17.240
Well, that's the best explanation I've heard.

270
00:18:17.500 --> 00:18:19.280
I also want to talk about Blockstream.

271
00:18:19.480 --> 00:18:21.120
You're the CEO and co-founder.

272
00:18:21.420 --> 00:18:22.660
Can you tell us about Blockstream?

273
00:18:23.380 --> 00:18:30.920
Yeah, so I mentioned that I was thinking in 2013 that Bitcoin is, well, I thought this at the beginning.

274
00:18:30.920 --> 00:18:33.060
Like in 2009.

275
00:18:33.380 --> 00:18:37.720
But one of the things I was thinking is that Bitcoin is not very private.

276
00:18:37.920 --> 00:18:41.860
So in 2013, I realized, okay, it's bootstrapping.

277
00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:42.860
People are going to use it.

278
00:18:42.980 --> 00:18:43.880
They are using it.

279
00:18:43.880 --> 00:18:47.760
And so I tried to read all the technical information.

280
00:18:48.760 --> 00:18:53.500
And I thought, well, maybe I can find a way to improve the privacy.

281
00:18:53.500 --> 00:19:02.000
Because I had a background in doing that kind of cryptography and reading those papers and implemented some of them in a library.

282
00:19:03.380 --> 00:19:06.340
So I spent some time trying to find a way to do that.

283
00:19:06.580 --> 00:19:10.420
And so I found a way to do it, which is now called Confidential Transactions.

284
00:19:10.420 --> 00:19:14.540
And so I thought maybe it could be added to Bitcoin.

285
00:19:14.760 --> 00:19:22.800
So I talked to the developers in IRC and realized that it's quite difficult to make changes to Bitcoin.

286
00:19:23.660 --> 00:19:33.300
So then I tried to find a way to make Bitcoin more modular so that you could do something permissionless without adding things to the main chain.

287
00:19:33.300 --> 00:19:37.660
And that's where the sidechain, like layer 2 idea, came from.

288
00:19:37.800 --> 00:19:41.760
But that then became quite complicated because it's a lot of things you need to implement.

289
00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:44.300
So I realized, okay, you're going to need a company.

290
00:19:45.260 --> 00:19:50.300
And so I raised money with venture capitalists to start Blockstream in 2014.

291
00:19:50.940 --> 00:19:53.880
The sidechain and Confidential Transactions was 2013.

292
00:19:54.180 --> 00:20:03.280
And then 2014, we'd got the first funding and recruited some of the developers who had been explaining answers.

293
00:20:03.300 --> 00:20:05.120
to my questions as I was learning.

294
00:20:05.800 --> 00:20:13.120
So the people that were doing a very technical programming on the Bitcoin internals, you need that skill set to make another layer.

295
00:20:13.660 --> 00:20:17.180
So I recruited some of them to join the company as well.

296
00:20:18.840 --> 00:20:23.600
And have you found really good, talented people in Taiwan this time?

297
00:20:24.580 --> 00:20:31.820
Yeah, there are definitely some people who are working on Bitcoin and sort of developer meetups and things like that.

298
00:20:31.820 --> 00:20:36.900
And the Technical University, NTUT, they have a lab.

299
00:20:37.140 --> 00:20:48.140
We have agreed to be a co-sponsor of the lab at the IT and Management Department with Computer Science students.

300
00:20:48.680 --> 00:20:52.400
And Plan B Network is the other co-sponsor with Tether.

301
00:20:52.400 --> 00:21:02.700
And so, yeah, I think we, you know, we'd be interested in people to submit CVs if they're interested in work.

302
00:21:03.080 --> 00:21:07.100
And in the future, we might be hiring technical people in Taiwan.

303
00:21:07.820 --> 00:21:11.740
And actually, we are quite international.

304
00:21:11.740 --> 00:21:24.240
So we have people who are working for Blockstream from home in Australia, New Zealand, Europe, Canada, America, everywhere, really.

305
00:21:24.440 --> 00:21:25.620
Even, you know, Japan.

306
00:21:25.880 --> 00:21:27.120
It's like all kinds of places.

307
00:21:27.320 --> 00:21:34.760
Wherever there's somebody who is good at a particular job that we need to get done, then we'll do that as well.

308
00:21:34.760 --> 00:21:44.740
But also, of course, in Taiwan, there are, you know, initiatives and business opportunities for real-world assets.

309
00:21:45.600 --> 00:21:47.900
And Liquid is a layer two.

310
00:21:48.140 --> 00:21:53.020
So people are probably more familiar with Lightning layer for Bitcoin, which is for sort of retail payments and micropayments.

311
00:21:53.780 --> 00:21:55.000
And Liquid is another layer two.

312
00:21:55.140 --> 00:21:56.980
So people use it for different things.

313
00:21:57.240 --> 00:22:01.860
They use it for a way, sort of an intermediate step between Bitcoin and Lightning.

314
00:22:01.860 --> 00:22:05.500
So there are Liquid wallets that are also Lightning wallets.

315
00:22:06.020 --> 00:22:07.500
And that works quite well.

316
00:22:08.060 --> 00:22:12.560
And they're using Boltz Exchange, which is a trustless service to swap.

317
00:22:13.480 --> 00:22:22.140
But Liquid is also heavily used for sort of financial transactions, shares and bonds and stable coins and things like that.

318
00:22:22.260 --> 00:22:29.260
So it's a whole ecosystem of companies and financial use cases and assets issued on the network.

319
00:22:29.260 --> 00:22:34.540
Actually, the network is operated by companies that are using it, basically.

320
00:22:35.020 --> 00:22:44.120
And so there are a mixture of companies, sort of wallets and APIs that use Bitcoin and crypto exchanges and actually, you know, a stock exchange.

321
00:22:44.480 --> 00:22:50.400
So quite a wide range of people using it and operating it.

322
00:22:50.400 --> 00:22:54.480
And members of this kind of liquid members group.

323
00:22:55.160 --> 00:23:10.560
So, yeah, I think that that's another reason that we can look to hire maybe more on a sort of business development or solution architects kind of role for these kind of use cases in Taiwan itself as well.

324
00:23:10.560 --> 00:23:13.300
Like people are going to be so excited.

325
00:23:14.500 --> 00:23:15.380
It's really late.

326
00:23:15.520 --> 00:23:16.260
Thank you for your time.

327
00:23:16.360 --> 00:23:17.600
But I have a last question.

328
00:23:17.740 --> 00:23:21.840
It's really important because as I walked in today, you are holding bubble tea.

329
00:23:22.040 --> 00:23:24.480
And I want to know what is your favorite flavor?

330
00:23:24.800 --> 00:23:26.860
Well, it's just tea with milk, basically.

331
00:23:27.500 --> 00:23:28.680
Just, oh, no bubbles?

332
00:23:29.000 --> 00:23:29.320
Bubbles.

333
00:23:29.460 --> 00:23:30.200
Bubbles, tea with milk.

334
00:23:30.200 --> 00:23:30.820
Of course, bubbles, yeah.

335
00:23:30.940 --> 00:23:31.920
Big one or small one?

336
00:23:32.380 --> 00:23:34.240
I think they're quite large, yeah.

337
00:23:34.240 --> 00:23:37.520
Thank you for your time and thank you for sharing.

338
00:23:37.880 --> 00:23:38.240
Of course.

339
00:23:38.640 --> 00:23:40.640
We hope to have you back sometime in the future.

340
00:23:41.320 --> 00:23:44.380
And then you can cut it.

341
00:23:44.900 --> 00:23:47.800
I mean, I'll just put it like here or here or something, right?

342
00:23:47.980 --> 00:23:48.160
Yeah, cool.

343
00:23:48.680 --> 00:23:51.920
And then I'll turn it on.

344
00:23:52.180 --> 00:23:54.300
And then if you get it quickly enough, you can see the screen.

345
00:23:59.420 --> 00:24:00.540
So now it's on the setup.

346
00:24:04.240 --> 00:24:34.220
Thank you.
